s back.\"** See \"Memory aid check\".\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1: the south pole of a magnet pulled away from a loop: current anticlockwise as seen from the magnet's side; the loop attracts the magnet. A2: explain with conservation of energy why the induced current cannot make a coil attract an approaching magnet. Crossed: A1 \"the loop repels the magnet\"; A2 \"because a coil always repels a magnet\".\n- **No numbers.** This video is qualitative by route and site; no calculation is added. The site's Q1 (north pole dropped towards a horizontal loop: anticlockwise from above) informs A1's style. Revision 2's scene 3 live control uses relative readouts only (× 1, × 2, × 3), not measured values.\n- **Handoff (route next).** Scene 13 replaces the magnet with a second coil connected to a supply and switch (switch open), the first coil still on the galvanometer, a gap between them, \"?\" (v12: \"How can one coil induce an emf in another without touching it?\"). See \"Final frame\".\n- **Colour key:** as v10: external field slate; induced field, induced current and the coil's induced poles rose; motion arrows amber outline; forces amber; energy bars: work amber, electrical orange, heat red.\n- **Colour roles (Revision 2, fixed for the whole video).** As the colour key, plus: the magnet's own poles ink on the plate's red and grey ends; step-rail numbers navy; relative readouts in the colour of their quantity (rose current, amber force, amber work, red heat); framed answers in a dark-ink frame; crossed slips and slip tags red (the \"what if\" stamp stays red because it marks the impossible case); hand-font tags in the colour of the quantity they mark.\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Current reasoning line (step-rail slot, feedback rows) at least 44 px (feedback rows 52 px; the framed Lenz statement 48 px); live readouts (scene 3 speed, current, force, work and heat; needle) about 52 px; inset labels and chips 30 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; 